# Fixture of the test suite. Not a design proposal.
#
# The one file that decides where the window's writing comes from (issue #85):
# a desktop theme that ships a `colors` file of its own hands both text colours
# out itself, and one that does not leaves them to the colour scheme. Until this
# story none of the bundled themes had one, so the assertion would have hung on
# whatever Plasma themes happened to be installed on the machine — of the eight
# on the customer's machine four bring a `colors` file and four do not, and a
# build host that installs only this project's KF6 parts brings none at all,
# because ksvg does not depend on libplasma.
#
# The narrow bundled theme deliberately has no such file — it is the other half
# of the assertion: no file, no theme colour, the colour scheme keeps its say.
#
# The two foreground values are deliberately colours no colour scheme carries.
# That is what makes the origin readable: a test that saw 35,38,41 could not
# tell a theme colour from a scheme colour that happens to look the same, and
# the assertion of AK 1 is about the origin and not about the value.
#
# Neither value colours the **hull** beside this file: the bundled SVGs name no
# `ColorScheme-*` class, so KSvg has nothing to substitute in them (measured —
# the drawn hull is byte for byte the one from before this file existed).
#
# That sentence used to cover the whole graphic and no longer can (issue #100,
# 07.08.2026). The window draws a second graphic since then, and that one is
# **not** bundled: no theme here ships `widgets/lineedit`, so KSvg falls back to
# `default` per image — and it recolours the fallback out of this file. Measured
# under this theme the field's surface renders 51,34,17, digit for digit the
# `BackgroundNormal` of `[Colors:View]` below.

[Colors:Window]
BackgroundNormal=17,34,51
ForegroundNormal=255,0,153
ForegroundInactive=0,153,255

# The second set carries **different** values on purpose, and since issue #100
# it has two jobs rather than one. Whoever changes a value here for the sake of
# the first job moves the second one with it, silently: `BackgroundNormal` is
# the colour the field graphic draws, and three assertions of #100 compare
# against it (`WideThemeFieldColour` in `capturetest.cpp`, written down twice on
# purpose so that a change here turns the suite red).
#
# The first job, unchanged: the window reads `[Colors:Window]`,
# the set its hull is drawn with. Without a second group the probe "reads the
# wrong group" could not be told apart from the probe "finds no file at all":
# both would end on the same `return {}`, produce the same five failing
# assertions, and one of the two would prove nothing (karpathy finding K1 to
# sprint 8). With these values the wrong group hands out a colour instead of
# nothing, and the two failure pictures differ.
[Colors:View]
BackgroundNormal=51,34,17
ForegroundNormal=0,204,0
ForegroundInactive=204,102,0
